\section*{Limitations}
\label{sec:limitations}

\paragraph{Cross-strategy deployment constraint.}
Cross-strategy transfer induces a d1/d2 boundary inversion under temperature sampling.
Per-strategy z-score normalization partially corrects the inversion under logistic regression (GPT-2~XL d1v2 AUC $0.24{\to}0.56$, OLMo $0.19{\to}0.70$) but yields negligible improvement for the Random Forest classifier used throughout the main experiments ($\Delta < 0.02$\,pp), and even in the best case (logistic regression) leaves a residual gap relative to within-strategy performance (${\sim}0.83$), indicating that a two-stage pipeline (strategy identification + strategy-conditioned classification) is needed for reliable cross-strategy deployment.

\paragraph{d0 inflation of headline metrics.}
Depth~0 (human-written text) is near-perfectly separable (recall $>$98\% across all nine model--strategy settings), inflating headline accuracy.
Cross-model transfer accuracy of 66.4\% is substantially driven by d0; d2 recall drops to 26.8\% in the worst case (Pythia holdout).

\paragraph{Controlled vs.\ ecological setting.}
All experiments use Wikipedia seed text with controlled recursive generation (LoRA fine-tuning, 1--1.5B parameter models).
This protocol isolates the recursive depth variable but diverges from wild recursive contamination, where models are pretrained on mixed corpora containing prior-generation outputs at unknown proportions; the fingerprints we identify may attenuate or shift under such diffuse exposure.
Depth signal is the dominant classification factor (5-fold CV 59--82\%, up to 2.5$\times$ chance): LOGO accuracy (72.1\%) matches standard CV (72.0\%), and seed-group prediction is at chance (10.2\% vs.\ 10\%; Appendix~\ref{app:domain_bias}).

\section{Generalization and Robustness}
\label{sec:generalization}

We test whether the atlas results (69.6--85.4\%) reflect genuine statistical regularities or training artifacts, evaluating four axes: classifier choice (\S\ref{sec:gen:baselines}), cross-model transfer (\S\ref{sec:gen:crossmodel}), cross-strategy transfer (\S\ref{sec:gen:crossstrategy}), and end-to-end learning (\S\ref{sec:gen:e2e}).

\subsection{Baseline Comparison}
\label{sec:gen:baselines}

Table~\ref{tab:baselines} evaluates three classifier families---bagging (RF), gradient boosting (XGBoost, LightGBM)---on the same 19 features across three atlas cells spanning the full accuracy range.
Per-cell spread is $\leq$0.4pp, establishing a feature-determined ceiling independent of the learning algorithm.
On Pythia nucleus, CORAL ordinal regression \cite{coral2020} (78.1\%) and DivEye \cite{diveye2026} (78.2\%) match the within-cell RF of Table~\ref{tab:main_matrix} within 0.1\,pp (bootstrap $p>0.05$, Bonferroni-corrected); all feature-based methods significantly outperform CE-MLP (74.9\%, $p<0.001$) and logistic regression (75.0\%).
Single-feature regression on mean perplexity performs at chance (25.1\%); two zero-shot LLMs---DeepSeek V3.2 (36.2\%, 95\% CI $[32.0, 40.4]$) and Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct (30.4\%, $[26.5, 34.6]$; each 500 samples, prompt template in Appendix~\ref{app:zeroshot_prompt})---both fail to distinguish recursive depth, performing near chance (25\%).
The accuracy gap between RF (78.2\%) and both LLMs exceeds 42\,pp---an order of magnitude above the CI half-width (${\approx}$4\,pp)---ensuring this finding is robust at $n{=}500$.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Classifier convergence: 4-class accuracy (\%) across three atlas cells. RF (bagging), XGBoost and LightGBM (gradient boosting) operate on the same 19 features; $\Delta_{\max}$: largest per-cell spread. Chance = 25\%. RF values differ from Table~\ref{tab:main_matrix} by $\leq$0.7pp due to matched-fold evaluation.}
\label{tab:baselines}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lcccc@{}}
\toprule
Cell & RF & XGB & LGBM & $\Delta_{\max}$ \\
\midrule
Pythia, nuc. & 78.8 & 79.0 & 78.9 & 0.2 \\
GPT-2~XL, $\tau$ & 85.8 & 86.2 & 86.2 & 0.4 \\
Pythia, top-$k$ & 68.9 & 68.9 & 68.7 & 0.2 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Convergence across five structurally different classifiers---spanning bagging, gradient boosting, ordinal regression, and ensemble detection---confirms the signal resides in the features, not the learning algorithm.
Figure~\ref{fig:confusion_matrix} shows d0 near-perfectly identified; errors concentrate at d2/d3 in-distribution and spread across d1--d3 under transfer.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_confusion_matrix.pdf}
\caption{Confusion matrices for the best in-distribution setting (GPT-2 XL, temp $\tau{=}0.9$, 85.4\%) and the best cross-model leave-one-out transfer (OLMo holdout, 69.2\%). d0 recall exceeds 99\% in both; errors spread across the d1--d3 range under transfer.}
\label{fig:confusion_matrix}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Cross-Model Transfer}
\label{sec:gen:crossmodel}

We perform leave-one-model-family-out evaluation: train on two families (nucleus only), test on the held-out third (Table~\ref{tab:cross_model}).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Cross-model transfer (leave-one-out, nucleus only). Chance = 25\% / 33\% (d1--d3). d1--d3 bal.\ acc: Pythia 49.9\%, OLMo 59.2\%, GPT-2~XL 57.2\% (mean 55.4\%).}
\label{tab:cross_model}
\begin{tabular}{lccccc}
\toprule
Holdout & Acc & d0 & d1 & d2 & d3 \\
 & (\%) & Rec. & Rec. & Rec. & Rec. \\
\midrule
Pythia & 62.4 & 100.0 & 49.3 & 26.8 & 73.6 \\
OLMo & \textbf{69.2} & 99.3 & 75.5 & 43.3 & 58.8 \\
GPT-2 XL & 67.6 & 98.5 & 74.7 & 52.6 & 44.5 \\
\midrule
Mean & 66.4 & 99.3 & 66.5 & 40.9 & 59.0 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Mean transfer accuracy is 66.4\% (2.7$\times$ chance); d0 recall ($>$98\%) inflates the headline while d2 drops to 26.8\% (Pythia holdout).
Excluding d0, d1--d3 balanced accuracy averages 55.4\% (chance = 33\%).

\subsection{Cross-Strategy Transfer}
\label{sec:gen:crossstrategy}

We train on two decoding strategies and test on the held-out third (Table~\ref{tab:cross_strategy}).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Cross-strategy transfer (leave-one-out). Chance = 25\% / 33\% (d1--d3). d0 recall $\geq$89\% inflates headline; d1--d3 mean: 45.9\%.}
\label{tab:cross_strategy}
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
Holdout & Pythia & OLMo & GPT-2 XL \\
 & Acc (\%) & Acc (\%) & Acc (\%) \\
\midrule
nucleus & \textbf{69.4} & \textbf{65.4} & \textbf{59.9} \\
temp ($\tau{=}0.9$) & 62.8 & 58.2 & 56.1 \\
top-$k$ ($k{=}50$) & 51.5 & 54.9 & 53.6 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Mean cross-strategy accuracy (59.1\%) falls 7pp below cross-model transfer (66.4\%); excluding d0, d1--d3 accuracy drops to 45.9\% (chance = 33\%).
d2 recall varies sharply: nucleus holdouts retain 30--54\%, top-$k$ holdouts fall to 8--19\%, and temperature holdouts span 3.7--39.5\% (median 8.9\%).
Training on nucleus + top-$k$ and testing on temperature inverts the d1/d2 boundary (d1v2 AUC $<$ 0.5); per-strategy z-score normalization partially recovers it but leaves a residual gap (Limitations).
A multi-temperature ablation (\S\ref{sec:gen:temperature}) confirms that within-$\tau$ depth signal holds for $\tau \leq 0.9$ (d1v2 AUC $>0.80$) but collapses at $\tau{=}1.1$; cross-$\tau$ LOO transfer (mean 46.7\%) is harder than cross-strategy transfer.

\subsection{End-to-End Learning}
\label{sec:gen:e2e}

DeBERTa-v3-base \cite{he2021_deberta_v3} and RoBERTa-base \cite{liu2019_roberta} finetuned end-to-end achieve 58.3\% ($\pm$4.7\%) and 59.5\% ($\pm$3.2\%) respectively---lagging the RF baseline (78.2\%) by 19.9pp and 18.7pp.
The 1.2pp inter-backbone gap rules out architecture bias; Table~\ref{tab:e2e_detail} shows both backbones share the same failure mode---high d0 F1 ($>$0.94) but collapsed d1/d2 performance (F1 $\leq$ 0.42)---while RF achieves 0.596 d2 recall, a $>$2$\times$ advantage at the hardest depth class.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Per-class performance for end-to-end PLM classifiers vs.\ RF baseline (Pythia-1.4B nucleus). PLM columns report F1; RF d2 column reports recall (the class with the largest PLM--RF gap). Both PLM backbones share the same failure pattern: collapsed d1/d2 scores despite adequate d0/d3.}
\label{tab:e2e_detail}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lccccc@{}}
\toprule
Method & Acc (\%) & d0 & d1 & d2 & d3 \\
\midrule
DeBERTa-v3 & 58.3\,$\pm$\,4.7 & .947 & .378 & .247 & .656 \\
RoBERTa-base & 59.5\,$\pm$\,3.2 & .958 & .416 & .261 & .647 \\
\midrule
RF (19 feat.) & 78.2 & --- & --- & .596$^\dagger$ & --- \\
\bottomrule
\multicolumn{6}{@{}l}{\footnotesize $^\dagger$Recall; shown for the depth class with the largest PLM--RF gap.}
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Feature space divergence.}
SHAP TreeExplainer analysis confirms that perplexity and surprisal features account for 88.8\% of RF's total contribution.
Three independent importance measures---SHAP, Gini, and permutation---show strong concordance (Kendall's $W{=}0.90$, $p{<}0.005$; pairwise Spearman $\rho$: SHAP--Gini $0.93$, SHAP--permutation $0.84$; Appendix~\ref{app:permutation}), with \texttt{p90\_ppl} appearing in the SHAP top-2 in 9/9 cells and \texttt{var\_surprisal} in 8/9.
Linear probing of PLM [CLS] representations yields negative $R^2$ for both \texttt{var\_surprisal} and \texttt{p90\_ppl} (Appendix~\ref{app:feature_overlap}), indicating that pretrained representations lack the linear structure needed to recover perplexity tail statistics---a systematic representational gap rather than a backbone-specific artifact.

DCOL \cite{cloc2025} reached 64.5\% (Appendix~\ref{app:dcol_iterations}); all end-to-end approaches (58--65\%) underperform feature-based methods, suggesting depth estimation benefits from targeted statistical features that capture distributional tail properties absent from PLM representations.
This comparison is bounded by backbone scale ($\leq$125M parameters); whether larger PLMs (e.g., 7B+) can close the gap via richer internal representations remains an open question.

\subsection{Temperature Robustness}
\label{sec:gen:temperature}

The cross-strategy results in \S\ref{sec:gen:crossstrategy} use a single temperature ($\tau{=}0.9$).
To test whether depth signal generalizes across temperatures, we repeat the classification pipeline at $\tau \in \{0.5, 0.7, 0.9, 1.1\}$ on Pythia-1.4B (full tables in Appendix~\ref{app:multi_tau}).

\paragraph{Within-$\tau$ robustness.}
For $\tau \in [0.5, 0.9]$, d1-vs-d2 AUC exceeds 0.80 (peak 0.851 at $\tau{=}0.7$, comparable to nucleus $p{=}0.95$'s 0.861) and 4-class accuracy ranges from 75.4\% to 77.3\%, confirming the depth signal is not an artifact of a single temperature.
The same top-5 features (\texttt{var\_surprisal}, \texttt{p90\_ppl}, \texttt{mean\_ppl}, \texttt{mean\_surprisal}, \texttt{p75\_ppl}) dominate across all $\tau$ values with only minor rank-order variation.

\paragraph{Collapse at $\tau{=}1.1$: memorization as prerequisite.}
At $\tau{=}1.1$, 4-class accuracy drops to 57.2\% and d1v2 AUC falls to 0.528.
Training loss reveals the mechanism: at low temperature ($\tau{=}0.5$), fine-tuning loss decreases steeply across recursive depths ($2.27 \to 0.42 \to 0.16$), indicating rapid memorization of the model's own output.
At $\tau{=}1.1$, loss remains nearly flat ($2.27 \to 1.91 \to 1.85$)---high-entropy output resists memorization, so depth-dependent distributional shifts do not accumulate.
This establishes \emph{memorization as a prerequisite} for detectable depth signal: recursive depth is discriminable only when successive fine-tuning iterations progressively reshape the output distribution.

\paragraph{Cross-$\tau$ non-transferability.}
Leave-one-$\tau$-out transfer yields mean accuracy of 46.7\% with mean d1v2 AUC of 0.319---worse than cross-strategy transfer (59.1\%; \S\ref{sec:gen:crossstrategy}).
Holdout $\tau{=}0.5$ produces inverted d1v2 AUC (0.176), confirming that different temperatures produce surprisal distributions with incompatible shapes.
This extends the strategy-specificity finding: even within a single decoding family, the feature space is temperature-specific, reinforcing the need for strategy-conditioned classifiers.

\paragraph{Ecological validity.}
On WikiText-103 (546 editorial passages), all three model-specific RF classifiers achieve $\geq$98.5\% d0 accuracy despite $+$621\% domain shift in mean perplexity---partly an easy case, as WikiText's higher perplexity places it farther from d1--d3 in feature space.
On noisier web-crawl data (5{,}000 C4 passages), FPR ranges from 4.7\% (OLMo) to 22.4\% (GPT-2~XL), concentrated in template/SEO content with high n-gram repetition (Table~\ref{tab:calibration}); deployment on heterogeneous web data requires model-specific calibration.

\subsection{Multi-Temperature Ablation}
\label{app:multi_tau}

To test whether the depth signal is an artifact of a single temperature setting, we repeat the within-strategy classification pipeline at four temperatures $\tau \in \{0.5, 0.7, 0.9, 1.1\}$ using Pythia-1.4B.

\paragraph{Within-$\tau$ classification.}
Table~\ref{tab:multi_tau_within} reports 4-class accuracy and pairwise depth AUCs for each temperature.
For $\tau \in [0.5, 0.9]$, d1-vs-d2 AUC exceeds 0.80 and 4-class accuracy ranges from 75.4\% to 77.3\%, confirming that the depth signal is not specific to $\tau{=}0.9$.
At $\tau{=}1.1$, accuracy drops to 57.2\% and d1v2 AUC falls to 0.528.
Training loss provides a mechanistic account: at $\tau{=}0.5$, loss decreases from 2.27 to 0.16 over three recursive iterations (rapid memorization), whereas at $\tau{=}1.1$ loss remains at 1.85 after the same iterations---the model fails to memorize its own high-entropy output, and depth-dependent distributional shifts do not accumulate.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\small
\caption{Within-$\tau$ 4-class classification (Pythia-1.4B, 5-fold CV). d$i$v$j$: pairwise AUC for depth $i$ vs.\ depth $j$.}
\label{tab:multi_tau_within}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lcccc@{}}
\toprule
$\tau$ & 4-class acc (\%) & d1v2 AUC & d0v1 AUC & d2v3 AUC \\
\midrule
0.5 & 77.3 & 0.842 & 0.996 & 0.764 \\
0.7 & 75.4 & 0.851 & 0.997 & 0.720 \\
0.9 & 75.9 & 0.808 & 0.991 & 0.781 \\
1.1 & 57.2 & 0.528 & 0.988 & 0.599 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Cross-$\tau$ transfer.}
Leave-one-$\tau$-out evaluation (Table~\ref{tab:multi_tau_cross}) yields mean accuracy 46.7\% with d1v2 AUC as low as 0.176, worse than cross-strategy LOO (59.1\%; \S\ref{sec:gen:crossstrategy}).
Different temperatures produce surprisal distributions with distinct shapes, making cross-$\tau$ transfer harder than cross-strategy transfer despite both involving the same model.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\small
\caption{Cross-$\tau$ leave-one-out transfer (Pythia-1.4B). Train on three temperatures, test on the held-out one.}
\label{tab:multi_tau_cross}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lcc@{}}
\toprule
Holdout & Acc (\%) & d1v2 AUC \\
\midrule
$\tau{=}0.5$ & 52.2 & 0.176 \\
$\tau{=}0.7$ & 41.0 & 0.299 \\
$\tau{=}0.9$ & 46.4 & 0.277 \\
$\tau{=}1.1$ & 47.1 & 0.525 \\
\midrule
Mean & 46.7 & 0.319 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

These results are based on a single model family (Pythia-1.4B); multi-model confirmation remains open.

